From ce3482bd1db2205be2a81b7d7fa2ec74a811d7f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mesh-LLM CI <ci@mesh-llm.local>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 02:04:42 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] skippy: add receipt recorder and replay

Receipt tensors are found from graph structure, with no architecture,
weight or node names: a classifier reads each layer of a built graph
between Skippy's block boundaries, using a per-ggml-op table of how
each op treats the token axis. It finds the layer's weight matmuls,
its token-mixing regions (attention, SSM and recurrent ops), the KV
or state rows they store, and the routers whose outputs feed a top-k
or argsort. A graph it cannot classify (an op missing from the table,
a layer reading an uncommitted value from another layer, more than 1%
of a layer's weights inside token mixing, a transposed V cache) gets
no receipt, with a reason. Stage realization moves the block
boundaries to the detached stage imports, so the classifier reads a
stage's first layer from the tensor the stage computes from.

skippy_model_set_receipt_recorder reports, after each computed ubatch,
the residual stream entering every stage layer, each layer's
token-mixing inputs, stored KV rows (read back from the cache in its
type) and token-mixing outputs, and the stage output, with the ubatch
geometry replay needs: tokens, sequences, positions, output rows, KV
cells, n_kv and the stage input rows. It also reports KV-cache
changes, so the caller can refuse sequences a replay cannot
reproduce. The receipt tensors are flagged as graph outputs in the
build and reserve graphs, so the planned allocation keeps them. On a
device that copies asynchronously, they are copied on the device into
a staging buffer right after the graph, and a copy thread brings them
to pinned host memory in small pieces while later ubatches compute,
so the decoding thread does not wait for the copy. Such a ubatch is
reported two ubatches later, before the next KV-cache change, or when
the caller calls skippy_model_flush_receipt_recorder; other devices
report it at once. Reports keep their order either way. Values the
host can read in place are read directly after one synchronization,
without the staging copy: a backend says which of its buffers those
are through ggml_backend_buffer_is_host_readable, which Metal exports
for its shared buffers.
A device whose backend exports ggml_backend_receipt_digest, as CUDA
does through ggml_backend_reg_get_proc_address, hashes the values
where they are instead: the recorder reports a 32-byte BLAKE3 digest
per column, and only the digests, the values in host memory and the
stage input reach the host.

skippy_model_replay_receipt replays recorded steps in a scratch
context created with the stage context's parameters, forcing the
recorded KV cells and n_kv, and reports the challenged layers in one
pass: for each, its committed tensors, every weight matmul's input and
output (with the experts of a mul_mat_id), its selections, and the
residual leaving it. Each step stops computing once those are done, so
a replay skips the later layers and the output head.
skippy_model_release_receipt_scratch frees the scratch context, which
holds a second KV cache, between replays.
skippy_model_seed_receipt_replay copies the K and V rows of given cells
from the stage context into the same cells of the scratch context, so
the next replay of a turn that continued a sequence, or started from a
cached prefix, runs only the turn's own steps on top of the cells it
attended to.
skippy_receipt_runtime_source names the llama.cpp commit and patch queue
the library was built from, as the prepared checkout records them, so
a receipt can name the runtime that computed it.

skippy_model_eval_receipt_glue lets an auditor compute one layer on
opened values: every weight matmul output, token-mixing output and
selection becomes an input holding the opened value, so the layer's
large weights are never read, and it reports what the layer computes
from them. skippy_model_eval_receipt_entry computes the residual
entering layer 0 from tokens, so an auditor can check that a committed
input of layer 0 is the token the receipt names.
skippy_receipt_stored_row_error compares a stored cache
row with a computed one against the computed row's own rounding into
the cache type.

Verifiable inference receipts commit to the residual stream and the
token-mixing values, and open a challenged layer by replay instead of
storing every matmul column. Replaces skippy_model_set_receipt_tap and
the serving context's eval-callback hooks it needed.
ABI 0.1.78 -> 0.1.91.
